Russian air and sea activity around UK  waters has increased dramatically in  recent years  it is back up at Cold War levels and it  is getting worse this is a resurgence  that has come very quickly it is an  intensifying resurgence of capability  and scale that we didn't necessarily see  coming maybe ten years ago in his first  major television interview the First Sea  Lord told me how the Royal Navy is now  establishing an official joint area of  operations in the North Atlantic it will  make the region a priority for Britain's  armed forces means ships and planes are  deployed there much more regularly in  future one of their tasks will be to  protect the vital undersea lines we are  aware that the Russians have the  capability to detect and work on those  cables and of course they're doing this  in very difficult environmental  conditions of very great depths it's not  easy to work out exactly what's going on  an estimated 97% of global  communications are transmitted by these  fiber-optic cables they run thousands of  meters below the sea right around the  world and carry 10 trillion dollars in  daily financial transactions if they  were cut it could be devastating the  very fact that they are going to where  we know the cables are and are using  vessels that are coming away from the  mothership in order to get close to the  cables is a sign that they are at the  very least surveying them and  potentially looking to be able to do  operations more than that NATO leaders  meet in Brussels this week Russia and  the threat to Europe will be high on the  agenda the Alliance's original founding  role securing the North Atlantic area is  once again the major priority Alister  bunka Sky News
 

